# LLMKube Metal Agent: NOPASSWD sudoers entry for /usr/bin/powermetrics.
#
# powermetrics requires root, but the Metal Agent runs as a regular user
# under launchd. This fragment grants the *minimum* privilege needed: the
# named user can run /usr/bin/powermetrics with EXACTLY the argument vector
# the agent uses, and nothing else.
#
# SECURITY: argv is pinned, not just the binary
# =============================================
# A common mistake is to grant `NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/powermetrics` (any args).
# That's a one-line root escalation: powermetrics has --output-file and other
# flags that, combined with root, give an attacker a write-anywhere primitive
# (`sudo powermetrics --output-file=/etc/passwd …`). The rule below names the
# one invocation the agent runs, the cpu_power+gpu_power sampler at a fixed
# sampling interval, and contains no wildcard characters, so it matches
# exactly one argument vector.
#
# This file is a template. Two placeholders are rendered at install time:
#
#   __LLMKUBE_USER__
#       The user the metal-agent runs as (typically the result of `whoami`
#       from the user's shell, NOT from a sudo session).
#   __LLMKUBE_POWERMETRICS_INTERVAL_MS__
#       The -i value the agent passes: its --apple-power-interval in whole
#       milliseconds, minimum 100. The agent default (1s) renders as 1000.
#
# A placeholder left in place, or an interval that differs from the agent's
# --apple-power-interval, means the rule does not match. The agent checks the
# rule with `sudo -n -l` before it starts sampling, logs "apple-power:
# refusing to start powermetrics" with the command to fix it, and the gauges
# read zero. Re-install with the matching interval whenever you change
# --apple-power-interval.
#
# If you upgrade the agent and powermetrics stops returning data, it likely
# means the agent's argv changed and this file is now a no-match. Re-install
# from the latest release.
#
# Install
# -------
#   make install-powermetrics-sudo                               # 1s default
#   make install-powermetrics-sudo APPLE_POWER_INTERVAL_MS=2000  # for 2s
#
# or by hand, from the repository root:
#
#   TMP=$(mktemp)
#   sed -e "s/__LLMKUBE_USER__/$(whoami)/" \
#       -e "s/__LLMKUBE_POWERMETRICS_INTERVAL_MS__/1000/" \
#       deployment/macos/sudoers.d/llmkube-powermetrics > "$TMP"
#   visudo -cf "$TMP"   # syntax check
#   sudo install -m 0440 -o root -g wheel \
#     "$TMP" /etc/sudoers.d/llmkube-powermetrics
#   rm "$TMP"
#
# Uninstall
# ---------
#   sudo rm /etc/sudoers.d/llmkube-powermetrics
#
# Why a separate file under sudoers.d
# -----------------------------------
# Keeps the grant out of the main /etc/sudoers and makes uninstall a single
# `rm`. Owner must be root:wheel and mode 0440 or sudo will refuse the file.

__LLMKUBE_USER__ ALL = (root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/powermetrics --samplers cpu_power\,gpu_power -i __LLMKUBE_POWERMETRICS_INTERVAL_MS__
